#[chapter/09/lore]{
  notes: [
    "Nine honks are nine sampled tropes: the Fool's set has sounded eight — three-wishes, trickster-in-the-tree, genie-in-the-bottle, vizier-at-court, two-receipts, labyrinth-city, sworn-oath,..."
    "The ninth honk was never sounded. Its shard is the egg: an unspent wish is an unsounded note."
    "Assembled, the set is a shape with one hole, egg-sized — the whole serial arranged around a held breath."
    "Jazz-geese fly in nines and honk in uneven time. Passing over the ridge, eight of their honks each rang a shard; the ninth found nothing to answer it. One goose taught the Fool his note, ..."
    "Chimney-swifts roost in the ridge flues and carry a sound a little farther than it meant to go: nothing said on the ridge stays on the ridge. They took the assembled hum down into the tow..."
    "The Fool keeps his set in a shard-rack of smoked ash — nine felt-lined slots, the ninth egg-shaped since before he knew why; for years it held a turnip so the rack would not look sad."
    "Chalked as a combine, the set is a round brace — everything inside must arrive together — with eight names joined by ampersands and a blank after the last. Eight pieces of a nine-piece co..."
    "One shard at a time: how the Fool says a set is learned. Eight shards rung together in uneven time are only weather; sounded one by one, each returned before the next is touched, they mak..."
  ]
}

